From 0270f4fa6c33468f7e2dcee728b334c948e03f87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vincent Leon <leon@mbp.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 12:41:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] Let a push registration carry provider-defined metadata
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The provider seam typed a registration as a single opaque string, because that
is what both channels in front of me were: an Expo token and an HMS token are
each one value with no structure.

UnifiedPush is not. A registration there is an endpoint URL plus the client's
p256dh public key and auth secret, because RFC 8291 encryption happens on the
sending side — the application server encrypts, so it needs the subscriber's
keys. Squeezing that into the token string would make every provider parse a
format only one of them uses.

So `deliver` now receives PushSubscription objects rather than bare tokens, and
the store keeps an optional per-registration metadata map alongside the expiry.
Contents stay opaque to everything except the provider that wrote them.

Renewal deliberately does not clear metadata: registration supplies it, and the
15s client heartbeat that renews the lease does not. Without that rule a
UnifiedPush subscription would lose its keys within a minute of being made.

On disk the metadata rides inside each entry, so an older daemon reading the
same PASEO_HOME still ignores the whole `providers` block as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
